@meta
id: 09-16
title: Drill — copy the address across
chapter: 09
type: drill
skills: op-motion
par: 20
minutes: 4
reps: 8
vscode: select the string, Ctrl-C, click between the quotes, Ctrl-V
open: mail.ts

@brief
Eight times: give `replyTo` the same address as `from`, by copying it.
Save, quit. Never type the address.

@keys
| `yt'` | yank up to, not including, the next quote |
| `p` | put it after the cursor |

@hints
Land on the first letter of the address and yank as far as the closing
quote.
--
`f'`, `l`, `yt'`. Then down to the empty pair, land on the opening quote
and `p`.

@solution
    f' l yt' j $ 2h p :wq<Enter>

Fifteen keystrokes, and the address is guaranteed to be identical to the
one above it. That guarantee is the reason to do it this way rather than
typing eighteen characters you think you remember.

`yt'` reads as a sentence: yank, till, quote. Change the operator and the
same sentence deletes it or changes it. Change the target character and
it works on a bracket, a comma or a colon instead. You are not learning
edits any more, you are learning to spell them.

@start mail.ts
const from = 'billing@globex.example';
const replyTo = '';

@expected mail.ts
const from = 'billing@globex.example';
const replyTo = 'billing@globex.example';
